{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "הואיל", :translit "ho'il", :gloss "he began / it was fitting"} {:he "כי הואיל ה' לעשות", :translit "ki ho'il hashem la'asot", :gloss "for the Lord undertook to make"} {:he "הואיל הלך אחרי צו", :translit "ho'il halach acharei tzav", :gloss "he began to walk after a command"} {:he "הואיל משה", :translit "ho'il mosheh", :gloss "Moses undertook"} {:he "הואיל ה' לעשות אתכם לו לעם", :translit "ho'il hashem la'asot etchem lo le'am", :gloss "the Lord undertook to make you for Him a people"} {:he "בניו של מקום", :translit "banav shel makom", :gloss "His children of the Place"} {:he "עובדי עכו\"ם", :translit "ovdei akum", :gloss "servants of idolatry"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :masorah, :count 3, :occurrences ["הואיל" "כי הואיל ה' לעשות" "הואיל הלך אחרי צו"]}], :frame {:text "Israel are called 'children of the Place' in multiple rabbinic passages. The expression is applied to Israel when they fulfill the will of God (Mishnah Sanhedrin 10:1 uses the idiom 'all Israel have a share in the world to come'). The term 'the Place' (המקום) as a divine epithet derives from Genesis 22:14 and is expounded in Berakhot 40a and Shabbat 10a. The verse 'כי הואיל ה' לעשות אתכם לו לעם' appears in Deuteronomy 26:18 and 2 Samuel 7:23.", :sources ["Mishnah Sanhedrin 10:1" "Genesis 22:14" "Berakhot 40a" "Shabbat 10a" "Deuteronomy 26:18" "2 Samuel 7:23"]}} {:quotes [{:he "את התורה הזאת לאמר", :translit "et ha-torah ha-zot lemor", :gloss "this Torah to say"} {:he "ה' אלהינו", :translit "Hashem Eloheinu", :gloss "the Lord our God"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Adjacency argument: the phrase immediately following 'את התורה הזאת לאמר' in Deut 1:6 is 'יהוה אלהינו,' from which he infers an emphasis on the unity of the divine Name."}], :frame {:text "The juxtaposition of verses to derive legal or theological lessons is a standard rabbinic hermeneutic. The phrase 'יהוה אלהינו' in Deuteronomy 1:6 immediately follows the statement that Moses began explaining the Torah, suggesting that the very first teaching was the unity of the divine Name. The obligation to affirm God's unity is rooted in Deuteronomy 6:4, 'יהוה אחד.'", :sources ["Deuteronomy 1:5-6" "Deuteronomy 6:4"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :masorah, :count 3, :occurrences ["הואיל" "כי הואיל ה' לעשות" "הואיל הלך אחרי צו"], :by :selah, :located [{:phrase "הואיל", :refs ["Deuteronomy 1:5" "1 Samuel 12:22" "Hosea 5:11"], :n 3, :lens :exact} {:phrase "כי הואיל ה' לעשות", :refs [], :n 0, :lens :plene-insensitive} {:phrase "הואיל הלך אחרי צו", :refs ["Hosea 5:11"], :n 1, :lens :exact}]} {:kind :other, :note "Adjacency argument: the phrase immediately following 'את התורה הזאת לאמר' in Deut 1:6 is 'יהוה אלהינו,' from which he infers an emphasis on the unity of the divine Name.", :by :selah, :ran? false}], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:33:23.078283760Z", :model "glm-5.2"}